course: dictionary
lesson: 8513
title: Satire: a joke with a point
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Satire is a way of writing, drawing or performing that uses humour, exaggeration or mockery to show that people, especially powerful people, are silly or wrong. A satire is one story, play or cartoon that does this. The word around it gives a clue: humour means it is meant to make you laugh.
explain: Look at the context clues. «Some sites, like The Onion, write satire: made-up news as a joke.» The words made-up news as a joke tell you that satire is not true news. It is a joke with a point.
explain: Satire can still fool people. «Satire is made-up news written as a joke; it is not lying when readers know it is a joke, but it can fool people who don't.» So ask yourself: does the reader know it is a joke? Check the label before you believe it.
explain: Satire is very old. «People have laughed at leaders for hundreds of years, with cartoons, impressions and fake news shows called satire.» Leaders are the powerful people that satire pokes fun at. Swap your meaning into the sentence: "people have mocked leaders for hundreds of years with jokes that show they are silly" still makes sense, so you have it.
fact: Satire means humour or mockery used to show that people, especially powerful people, are silly or wrong.
fact: A satire can be a story, a play, a cartoon or a comedy show.
fact: «Satire» is made-up news written as a joke.
fact: Gulliver's Travels and Animal Farm are famous satires.
fact: A satire website writes made-up funny news, so do not believe it as real news.
exercise: «Some sites, like The Onion, write satire: made-up news as a joke.» What is satire here? | A recipe for cakes | A map of the world | *Made-up news written as a joke | Real news checked by experts
exercise: A satire website says a leader ate the moon for lunch. What is the best reason to check it? | Satire is always true, so it must be real | Leaders always eat moon rock | Jokes never have any words in them | *Satire sites write made-up funny news, so it may be a joke
exercise: «Satire is not lying when readers know it is a joke.» What makes satire not lying? | It is printed in a newspaper | It is always about sport | It has no words in it | *Readers know it is a joke
test: Why can satire fool people? | *Some readers do not know it is a joke | It is always written in capital letters | It has no humour at all | It only uses numbers
test: Which two are famous satires? | A dictionary and a atlas | *Gulliver's Travels and Animal Farm | A weather forecast and a recipe | A bus timetable and a shopping list
exam: What is the difference between satire and a real news report? | *Satire is a joke with a point, while a real report tells what happened | They are both cartoons with no words | Satire is always true, and a real report is always a joke | They are both made-up stories with no point
